Related works of HSSP HSSP has appeared under various names across
different research areas. Besides being referred to as the multiset recovery
problem in studies on the multi-solution nature of (n, k)-complete HSSP,
it is also a special instance of the generating set problem [2] in combi-
natorial number theory. The generating set problem has been studied in
various contexts, with one notable approach by Collins et al. proving its
NP-completeness [2]. Some greedy heuristic algorithms have been proposed
to address it [2, 17]. More recent works have explored connections between
HSSP and graph theory, including Busson et al.’s transformation of the Wi-Fi
access point channel allocation problem into a 1-extendable partition prob-
lem [5], which is equivalent to the generating set problem.
    In cryptography, the HSSP over the ring of residues modulo M has been
applied to public-key cryptosystems, improving the efficiency of key genera-
tion through the BPV generator [1]. Subsequently, Nguyen and Stern (1999)

                                       4
proposed the NS algorithm, based on orthogonal lattice attacks, to solve these
instances of HSSP [18]. Gini and Coron followed their work and enhanced
the NS algorithm, obtaining a polynomial-time probabilistic version [19–21].
However, these methods rely on favorable properties of the matrix A and
vector B, making them less effective for solving the (n, k)-complete HSSP.
    Recent studies in AI privacy have linked HSSP to federated learning [3,4].
While techniques like differential privacy, homomorphic encryption, and se-
cure multi-party computation protect intermediate data [22], few address the-
oretical privacy analysis from the shared updates. For example, [3] mapped
the input data of the federated K-means clustering algorithm to a hidden
multiset in HSSP, formulating intermediate results as subset sums. It trans-
forms the input recovery attack into an instance of HSSP, connecting AI
privacy with HSSP.
    Unlike the aforementioned heuristic and probabilistic approaches, our
work focuses on developing deterministic algorithms for solving the (n, k)-
complete HSSP, offering a more structured and reliable solution framework.

Organization The structure of the paper is as follows. Section 2 provides
some necessary background. In Section 3, we present an optimized version of
the brute-force search algorithm for solving the (n, k)-complete HSSP. Section
4 discusses the proposed algorithm based on symmetric polynomials theory
and Vieta’s formulas in detail. Conclusion and future work are outlined in
Section 5.


