3           Brute-force search
Brute-force search, also known as exhaustive search, is a general problem-
solving technique that systematically examines all possible candidates. For
the (n, k)-complete HSSP, we know that each sum is the sum of k elements
from X, but we do not know which k elements contribute to each sum. If we
simply traverse all possible subsets of X and check the subset sums, such a
brute-force approach can be extremely time-consuming.
     Hence, we might as well fix an invertible binary matrix An whose rows
each contain    exactly k ones, and select n possible subset sums from the
total nk subset sums to identify the candidates of X. There are totally
