4.5       Nontrivial permutation-symmetric subset of Xn,k
This method appears to be applicable to any permutation-symmetric subset of
Xn,k , where permutation-symmetric means the subset of Xn,k remains invariant
under any permutation of x1 , x2 , . . . , xn . However, we claim that Xn,k does not
contain a nontrivial permutation-symmetric subset in which every element of X
contributes to the subset sums. If some elements of X do not contribute to the
subset sums of a subset of Xn,k , the subset might exhibit symmetry. However, we
do not consider such cases, as they can be effectively treated as instances with a
smaller n.

Theorem 4. Let Xn,k be the multiset of all k-subset sums of finite multiset X =
{x1 , x2 , . . . , xn } of cardinality n, then Xn,k does not have nontrivial permutation-
symmetric subset with every element of X contributing to the subset sums.

Proof. Suppose B is such a permutation-symmetric subset of Xn,k . Since B is
not empty, there exists xi1 + xi2 + · · · + xik ∈ B. And since B 6= Xn,k , there
exists xi′1 + xi′2 + · · · + xi′k ∈         / B. By the symmetry property, we could arbitrarily
exchange the positions of any two elements of X. For any j = 1, 2, . . . , k, if
{i1 , . . . , ik } ∩ {i′1 , . . . , i′k } = ∅, exchange xij and xi′j , then xi′1 + xi′2 + · · · + xi′k ∈ B,

                                                   20
which is a contradiction. If {i1 , . . . , ik }∩ {i′1 , . . . , i′k } is not empty, keep the indexes
in the intersection set unchange, and exchange the other indexes. Then we could
still obtain a contradiction.


4.6      The homogeneous symmetric polynomial rings
Fixing n and k, we compute Pu recursively from u = 1 to n as described above.
However, the Matrix (1) may be non-invertible for certain values of u. In such
cases, it is not possible to uniquely determine the value for any one partition of
u without additional information in Algorithm 2, leading to the failure of the
algorithms. This raises the following question:

Question 1. When the Matrix (1) becomes non-invertible for certain values of u,
can we still uniquely determine the values for the partitions of v where v ≥ u by
modifying Algorithm 2 without additional information?

   Before answering this question, let us first consider the homogeneous symmetric
polynomial ring of degree u, denoted here by Ru , and prove the following theorem.

Theorem 5. For a positive integer of u > 1 and the homogeneous symmetric
polynomial ring Ru of degree u over R. A basis of Ru can be constructed by Su
and the products of the elements of Rv , v < u if and only if Matrix (1) is invertible.
This is is equivalent to
