                                 X
                                       j−1 u−1    n
                                   (−1) j              6= 0.
                                                 k−j
                                 j=1

Proof. The ring Ru can be taken as a linear space over R, whose dimension is equal
to the number of partitions of u. Matrix (1) is taken as the transformation ma-
trix from the vector (Pu , P(1,u−1) , . . . , P(1,1,...,1) ) to the vector (Su , P1 Pu−1 , . . . , P1u ).
The former vector always forms a basis of Ru . For the latter vector, except the
first component, the other components are computed by the homogeneous sym-
metric polynomials of lower degree. From the structure of Matrix (1), it could be
observed that {P1 Pu−1 , . . . , P1u } are linearly independent. At this time, we could
not obtain any new item, that is linearly independent of {P1 Pu−1 , . . . , P1u }, from
Rv , v < u. And Su is linearly independent from {P1 Pu−1 , . . . , P1u } if and only if
Matrix (1) is invertible. Thus, {Su , P1 Pu−1 , . . . , P1u } form a basis of Ru if and only
if Matrix (1) is invertible. Proof of this theorem is thus complete.

    Back to Question 1, the primary available data is the set Xn,k , which contains
all k-subset sums of n elements. Consequently, Su can be uniquely determined for


