any u ∈ Z>0 . Then, starting from u = 1 to larger values, we can get the values
Pp of all partitions. Therefore, we can assume Rv ’s are known for all v < u before
computing Ru . The proof of Theorem 5 shows that choosing {P1 Pu−1 , . . . , P1u } is
the same as choosing other products of the elements of Rv , v < u to form a basis.
Hence, {Su , P1 Pu−1 , . . . , P1u } is chosen to compute Pp , where p is a partition of u.
If we can not determine the value of any Pp of degree u uniquely with this choice,
neither could the others.
    Furthermore, if there exists 1 < u0 < n such that Matrix (1) is non-invertible,
then a basis of Ru0 cannot be constructed from Su for all u ≤ u0 . At this time,
can a basis of Ru , where u ≥ u0 , be constructed from Sv for all v ≤ u? To answer
this question, we have the following theorem.

Theorem 6. If there exists 1 < u0 < n such that Matrix (1) is non-invertible
when u = u0 and is invertible for all u < u0 , then a basis of Ru , where u < u0 ,
can be constructed from Sv , v < u0 . But a basis of Ru , where u ≥ u0 , cannot be
constructed from Sv , v ≤ u.

Proof. The conclusion for the cases where u ≤ u0 follows from Theorem 5. We
